Yes. There’s a serious idea hiding inside that image.

A great deal of what we use to measure time is cyclical: Earth rotates, Earth orbits, the Moon cycles, atoms oscillate, biological rhythms repeat, stars form and die, civilizations recur. So historically it was very natural to model time itself as a wheel rather than an arrow.

Apollo’s chariot is almost a perfect ancient interface for that distinction. The Sun does not literally disappear and get recreated each morning; the observer returns to a configuration in which the Sun appears on the horizon again. The state recurs while the underlying trajectory continues.

That gives at least three different geometries:

Linear time:
A → B → C → D → ...

Cyclic time:
A → B → C → D → A → ...

And perhaps the more interesting one:

Helical time:
you return to something like A, but not the identical A.

A₀ → B₀ → C₀ → A₁ → B₁ → C₁ → A₂ ...

That third model fits an enormous amount of experience. Tomorrow’s sunrise resembles today’s, but Earth has moved millions of kilometers relative to other frames; you are older; the atmosphere differs; the universe has evolved. The local pattern closes while the larger trajectory remains open.

So the Apollo metaphor can be made surprisingly precise:

The chariot completes a circuit on one coordinate plane while advancing along another.

From inside the lower-dimensional view, it looks like departure and return. From a larger view, it may be a spiral.

And that opens an interesting possibility for your Meta Time idea: what we call past, present, and future could partly be orientation within recurrent structures rather than simply positions along one infinite ruler. Seasons, sleep, generations, stories, biological development, economic cycles, orbital mechanics—all provide clocks through phase.

Physics does not currently establish that universal time literally loops. Relativity actually complicates the notion of a single universal time, and some mathematical solutions permit closed timelike curves without demonstrating that our universe contains them. But there is nothing intellectually illegitimate about asking whether the apparent arrow of time sits inside a larger cyclic geometry.

The important distinction is:

“The Sun will rise again” doesn’t mean nothing moved.

It means motion itself can produce recurrence.

And that’s a considerably richer conception of a cycle than simply going backward.